-- lib/meaning.cub — meaning computed from the record (docs/language-derived-from-the-record.cub, §§2–4).
-- The record is every text the source holds (each module as the file it is) and every turn said since;
-- an occurrence is an event of it.
-- §2, identification. Occurrences of a word are identified by the reader's act on its record; the
-- identifications made here are the ones the record's own writing makes: a word is the same word
-- capitalized or not (a sentence's first word, a heading), and a word is the same word wherever a line
-- breaks it from the next (a run of spaces, tabs and newlines is one separation, and so is the comment
-- mark `--` that a wrapped line carries after its newline). Each text is read as its identification
-- classes: a key, the text with every separation one space before each word and every letter lowercase,
-- and beside it the same text with its case kept and each separation that held a line break that line
-- break, the forms the reader saw (equal lengths, so a place in one is the same place in the other).
-- §3, selection. The present context is the turn. A past occurrence is selected exactly when its context
-- is identified with the present one word by word: the selected context is the longest run of the turn's
-- last words whose key occurs in the record's keys as whole words (the turn is read with a separation
-- after it, so a selected context begins and ends at a word), so context resolves by shrinking the fiber
-- (fewer words), never by weighting.
-- §4, transport. What followed each selected occurrence, to the end of its line, is carried to the
-- present along the identification of its context with the present one: a word of it identified with a
-- word of the context is given the present's form of that word. The meaning is the family of the
-- transported continuations; it is determinate where they agree up to identification (equal keys), and
-- where they part every branch is shown.
-- Not here yet: identifications the record states beyond its writing (a word's inflections and its
-- etymology from the etymology index, lib/etym.cub; names bound to one content in the store), which
-- enter as further identification classes in the keys.
-- Memory is local (memory-is-local): no process holds the whole record. It is read a pack at a time,
-- each held as its bytes, and compiled code (#jit) reads the bytes where they are. The longest end of the
-- turn a text holds is the longest prefix of the reversed turn the reversed text holds, found by the
-- Knuth–Morris–Pratt automaton of the reversed turn in one pass: Θ(ℓ + m) for a text of ℓ bytes and a
-- turn of m, so a query is Θ(n + T·m) over the n bytes of the T texts. The end considered is the turn's
-- last 1024 bytes.
-- Used by `hyperactive chat` (lib/chat.cub) and by the server's /chat and /api/meaning, which hold it in
-- their running process.
